architecture: `

<section class="domain-architecture-panel"> <h2>How This Topic Is Organised</h2>

<p>This domain is organised around a central question: how should biology understand living systems as viability-oriented organised persistence?</p>

<p>APS approaches this question through a sequence of interdependent concepts. Life is the phenomenon requiring explanation. Agency concerns the activity through which living systems maintain themselves. Viability provides the normative reference point for that activity. Organisation concerns the structured relations that sustain persistence. Persistence concerns the continuity of organised living systems through time. Scale concerns the distributed coordination of biological organisation across space and time. Biological explanation seeks to understand how these processes are generated, maintained, and transformed.</p>

<p>The anchor articles establish the central APS understanding of life and agency. The remaining articles develop this framework through five connected explanatory pathways.</p>

<h3>Life and Agency</h3>

<p>These articles establish the APS understanding of life, agency, organisms, and biological systems. They explain why living systems are distinguished not by particular materials or mechanisms but by viability-oriented organised activity.</p>

<h3>Organisation and Constraint</h3>

<p>These articles develop the organisational architecture of life. They explain how biological organisation, constraints, and constraint closure contribute to the maintenance of persistence.</p>

<h3>Persistence and Temporality</h3>

<p>These articles explore how living systems maintain continuity through continual transformation. Persistence, process, temporality, and continuity are presented as central dimensions of biological explanation.</p>

<h3>Scale and Normativity</h3>

<p>These articles explain how biological organisation is coordinated across interacting scales and why viability generates biological normativity, function, and evaluative organisation.</p>

<h3>Conceptual Integration and Biological Explanation</h3>

<p>These articles synthesise multiple APS concepts into a unified explanatory framework. They clarify what biology explains, how biological explanation differs from physical explanation, and how continuity is organised across the architecture of life.</p>

<h3>Suggested Reading Pathway</h3>

<p>Readers new to APS should begin with Agency as the Defining Activity of Life and What Is Life? — A Biological Question Revisited. These anchor articles establish the central orientation of the framework.</p>

<p>Readers should then move to Biological Agency as the Activity of Self-Maintenance, Viability, Biological Organisation, and Biological Explanation. From there, readers can explore persistence, temporality, constraint organisation, scale, normativity, and organismal organisation before moving to the synthesis articles that integrate these themes into a broader understanding of biological explanation.</p>

</section> `,

