  architecture: `
<section class="domain-architecture-panel">
  <h2>How This Topic Is Organised</h2>

  <p>This domain is organised around a central question: how do living systems maintain organised persistence through ongoing relations with their environments?</p>

  <p>APS treats ecology as a continuity architecture rather than as the study of organisms and environments considered separately. Living systems do not merely occupy environments. They persist through continuous coupling with ecological conditions that both constrain and enable viability.</p>

  <p>The anchor articles establish the APS account of ecology as organised persistence across ecological relations and scales. They explain why organisms and environments cannot be understood independently and why ecological organisation forms an essential dimension of biological explanation.</p>

  <p>The remaining articles develop this framework through four connected explanatory pathways.</p>

  <h3>Ecological Organisation</h3>

  <p>These articles establish the conceptual foundations of ecology in APS. They explain why ecological organisation concerns the maintenance of viability through relations extending beyond the boundaries of individual organisms.</p>

  <h3>Organism–Environment Coupling</h3>

  <p>These articles examine the reciprocal relations through which organisms and environments co-produce one another. Ecological persistence is not imposed from outside the organism, nor generated entirely from within it. Instead, viability emerges through ongoing coupling between living systems and the ecological conditions they inhabit.</p>

  <h3>Ecology Across Scale</h3>

  <p>These articles explain how ecological organisation extends across multiple levels of biological organisation. Persistence depends upon coordinated relations distributed across organisms, populations, communities, ecosystems, and broader ecological contexts.</p>

  <h3>Resilience and Continuity</h3>

  <p>These articles explore how ecological systems maintain continuity despite disturbance, change, and uncertainty. APS interprets resilience not simply as stability but as the capacity of ecological organisation to reorganise while preserving viability.</p>

  <h3>Suggested Reading Pathway</h3>

  <p>Readers new to this domain should begin with The Ecological Organisation of Life and Ecology as Organised Persistence Across Scales. These articles establish the APS understanding of ecology as the study of persistence through organism–environment relations.</p>

  <p>Readers should then move to Why Organisms Cannot Be Understood Apart from Their Environments, Environment, Coupling, and Agency, and Organism–Environment Coupling, which develop the central APS account of ecological organisation. Ecological Organisation and Organised Persistence integrates these themes into a unified framework. Organism–World Coupling clarifies common misunderstandings concerning agency and ecological interaction, while Resilience, Ecology, and Continuity explores the implications of ecological organisation for persistence under changing conditions.</p>
</section>
