  architecture: `
<h2>How This Topic Is Organised</h2>

<p>The Cognition and Mind corpus is organised through six connected explanatory relationships.</p>

<h3>Definition and Boundary</h3>
<p>These articles establish what APS means by cognition and where cognitive explanation belongs within biology. They distinguish cognition from life, agency, regulation, signalling, adaptive responsiveness, and other forms of biological organisation that may remain non-cognitive.</p>

<h3>Biological Dependencies</h3>
<p>These articles examine biological agency, evaluation, significance, integration, organised persistence, and related conditions upon which cognition may depend. These dependencies help explain how cognition can arise within living organisation, but their presence is not by itself evidence that cognition is present.</p>

<h3>Attribution and Boundary Controls</h3>
<p>These articles govern how cognitive language is used and compared across biological systems. Shared terminology, behavioural complexity, non-neural organisation, and similarities with familiar cognitive phenomena can motivate investigation, but cognitive attribution requires independently warranted organisational and explanatory evidence.</p>

<h3>Implementation and Localisation</h3>
<p>Once cognition has been independently warranted, these articles ask where and how the relevant cognitive organisation is materially realised. Distributed biological organisation does not itself establish distributed cognition. The explanatory sequence is cognitive warrant → material implementation → organisational distribution → explanatory consequence → comparator test.</p>

<h3>Applications and Tests</h3>
<p>These articles apply the cognition framework to particular organisms, biological phenomena, and contested cases. Their purpose is to test whether cognitive explanation is warranted relative to the strongest adequate non-cognitive alternatives rather than to assume cognition from biological complexity alone.</p>

<h3>Downstream Questions and Interfaces</h3>
<p>These articles examine mind, meaning, representation, consciousness, sentience, selfhood, reflective agency, and related phenomena. Evidence for cognition does not by itself establish these downstream capacities.</p>

<h3>Suggested Reading Pathway</h3>
<p>Begin with the definition and biological boundary of cognition, then examine its biological dependencies and the controls governing cognitive attribution. From there, proceed to implementation and localisation, applications such as plant cognition, and finally the downstream questions concerning mind and related phenomena.</p>
`,

