    architecture: `
  <section class="domain-architecture-panel">
    <h2>How This Topic Is Organised</h2>

    <p>This topic asks how living systems develop forms of organisation that extend beyond individual organisms. Social organisation arises through interactions among participants whose activities become coordinated, sustained and transmitted over time. APS examines how these relationships contribute to organised persistence without assuming that every social system is itself a biological agent.</p>

    <h3>Communication and Coordination</h3>

    <p>Social organisation begins with relationships among participants. Communication, cooperation and coordination allow activities to become connected and mutually dependent. Symbolic coordination extends these relationships through shared signs, meanings and practices, making more complex and enduring forms of social organisation possible.</p>

    <h3>Norms, Values and Social Regulation</h3>

    <p>Social relationships are shaped by expectations, norms and shared evaluations. These can stabilise behaviour and coordinate activity across individuals and groups. APS examines their contribution to social organisation while distinguishing the persistence of a social arrangement from its moral legitimacy or desirability.</p>

    <h3>Culture, Inheritance and Continuity</h3>

    <p>Social organisation extends through time as practices, knowledge, expectations and patterns of coordination are maintained and transmitted. Cultural inheritance allows forms of organisation to persist beyond the lives of particular participants, while remaining open to modification and historical change.</p>

    <h3>Institutions and Technology</h3>

    <p>Institutions stabilise social relationships through established roles, rules and practices. Technology provides material means through which activities can be coordinated, recorded and extended. Together, these help explain how social organisation can become durable and distributed across people, artefacts and generations.</p>

    <h3>Morality, Ethics and Wider Applications</h3>

    <p>Social organisation provides an important context for moral evaluation, but social coordination and moral justification are not the same thing. The articles on morality and ethics examine this distinction and its implications. Further articles explore how APS concepts can be applied to culture, economics and the social sciences, without assuming that these applications establish explanatory superiority over existing approaches.</p>

    <h3>Suggested Reading Pathway</h3>

    <p>Begin with <a href="/articles/social-organisation/"><em>Social Organisation</em></a> for an introduction to the domain. Then explore <a href="/articles/communication-and-coordination/"><em>Communication and Coordination</em></a>, <a href="/articles/symbolic-coordination/"><em>Symbolic Coordination</em></a> and the articles on social norms. Continue through <a href="/articles/cultural-organisation/"><em>Cultural Organisation</em></a>, <a href="/articles/cultural-inheritance/"><em>Cultural Inheritance</em></a> and <a href="/articles/institutions/"><em>Institutions</em></a> to understand how social relationships acquire continuity. The articles on human values, moral evaluation, morality and ethics develop the evaluative questions arising within social life. Finally, explore the integrative and specialised articles for broader connections with biology and the social sciences.</p>

    <p>The articles and glossary entries below provide the available APS treatments of these subjects. The suggested pathway is a guide to reading, not a claim that social organisation always develops through a fixed sequence or that social persistence establishes moral legitimacy.</p>
  </section>
