  architecture: `
    <section class="domain-architecture-panel">
      <h2>How This Topic Is Organised</h2>

      <p>This topic addresses a practical scientific question: how can we obtain evidence for the biological organisation described by APS?</p>

      <p>APS understands living systems in terms of viability-oriented organised persistence. Diagnosis and empirical investigation ask how the relevant organisation can be recognised, examined and tested in actual biological systems. A conceptual account of life does not by itself establish that a particular system possesses the organisation it describes.</p>

      <h3>Recognising Living Organisation</h3>

      <p>This pathway concerns the evidence used to identify living systems and distinguish them from non-living or life-like systems. Observable properties, biological activity and proposed biosignatures provide evidence for investigation, but their significance depends upon what they establish about the organisation of the system.</p>

      <h3>Evidence and Empirical Assessment</h3>

      <p>This pathway examines how claims about biological agency, viability, organisation and persistence can be connected to observations and tests. It distinguishes a theoretical description from evidence that the proposed organisation is materially realised and contributes to continued functioning.</p>

      <h3>Perturbation and Biological Dependencies</h3>

      <p>This pathway considers what happens when the conditions supporting a living system are altered. Perturbations can help identify dependencies by revealing which processes and organisational relations contribute to maintaining or restoring viable functioning. Their interpretation requires appropriate controls and consideration of alternative explanations.</p>

      <h3>Malfunction, Failure and Diagnosis</h3>

      <p>This pathway examines how disruption, malfunction and failure can illuminate biological organisation. When a system loses a capacity or fails to maintain its functioning, the resulting changes may help identify the dependencies upon which persistence relies. Failure alone, however, does not establish the particular explanation of that failure.</p>

      <h3>Life Detection and Boundary Cases</h3>

      <p>This pathway considers how evidence should be assessed in difficult cases, including minimal, unfamiliar or apparently life-like systems. The central question is not whether a system resembles familiar organisms, but whether the available evidence warrants attributing the relevant form of living organisation.</p>

      <h3>Suggested Reading Pathway</h3>

      <p>Begin with the questions of how life is recognised and what counts as evidence for living organisation. Then examine empirical assessment, perturbation and biological diagnosis to understand how proposed organisational dependencies may be investigated. Finally, explore biosignatures, life detection and borderline cases, where the distinction between suggestive indicators and warranted biological conclusions becomes especially important.</p>

      <p>The articles and glossary entries listed below provide the available APS treatments of these subjects. The pathways are guides to reading and investigation, not claims that every proposed diagnostic procedure has already been empirically validated.</p>
    </section>
  `,

